@page rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · CELESTE · STANDING" m="TKC: TL · DR3 · DOM; VOL. I · {v1:forms} · {v1:designations} · {v1:household}"
# The clerk's version and the true one
rec:
On the ledger | Unbound, sixty-nine, head of a single-family household in the outer district. Never Bound, so never widowed in law. A woman with no husband, no office and no standing beyond what the reclassification granted her in writing (TL).
In the widows' row | She sits in the row reserved for widows at Temple, and nobody has ever asked her to move. Minister Bell has known her thirty-four years, eaten her sweet potato pie at harvest suppers, and called every winter when the Temple needed coats repaired. He greets her with both hands out. She looks at their joined hands and answers "Morning," and he keeps smiling (DR3).
The six-year filing | When Miriam left in 2090 she filed to reclassify the household under her own registration. Approval took six years, because she had to prove the arrangement was permanent, which meant proving her daughter wasn't coming back. She put on her one good blouse, went downtown, and said what she needed to say (TL).
The authority line | A form for Solomon came demanding an adult male authority. She wrote "deceased" on the line. It came back with the word circled and a request for the next adult male, and Zion, at eighteen, signed. She hadn't wanted him to (DR3).
INFERENCE | She has no office, no money and no husband, and she may be the most powerful person in her district.
prompt: What this household lives on, who earns it, and what it doesn't cover | fill

@page rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · CELESTE · FAITH AND SCRIPTURE · 1 OF 2" m="TKC: DOM · DR3 · TL · AM · FR 16; VOL. I · {v1:teaches} · {v1:lived} · {v1:lived6}"
# What she believes
Celeste is the most religious person in the book and the least Imperial (AM). Those don't pull against each other for her, and the pages where a reader feels that are the engine of the Parish house.
rec:
What she believes | INFERENCE: Keep part of yourself unregistered. It's what the salt water is for, what the second name is for, and what "morning" is instead of an answer. Faith can survive an institution by not handing it everything.
Who taught her | OPEN QUESTION: Her grandmother, who cried at the signs (TL), or her mother, who lost her name to a Commonwife marriage? The files don't say who first put scripture in her hands.
What she can recite | All of it, including the Covenant of Purpose (AM). INFERENCE: Fluency is her camouflage.
What she trusts when frightened | Her hands. She prays from the moment they leave the house, thumb against her first knuckle, one tap per petition (DR3). When Eden paid the ten dollars she didn't pray about it. She cleared a shelf (DR3).
What she'd be arrested for | The Salt Ceremony, which the Empire doesn't know exists, and burying the dead the old way (DOM). The real name of the city, said out loud to Eden the night before she leaves (TL).
empire: What the Temple teaches about a woman like her
An Unbound woman is "difficult and surveilled" (TL), and every household form asks for a man (DR3). POSSIBLE: The Temple seats her in the widows' row and preaches 1 Timothy 5.5, the "widow indeed" who "trusteth in God, and continueth in supplications and prayers." It honors her and files her in one gesture, without ever saying she was never anyone's widow.
/end
small: SUPERSEDED: An earlier round had Celeste knowing Miriam was alive and hiding it. She has no proof Miriam is alive (FR 16), so her silence about her daughter is grief with no information in it.
prompt: The one piece of Imperial doctrine she half believes, and hates that she does | fill

@page rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · CELESTE · FAITH AND SCRIPTURE · 2 OF 2" m="2 TIM 1.5; RUTH 1–4; 2 SAM 21.8–14; MATT 5.13; PS 137.4; MATT 6.6; ISA 10.1–2; LUKE 18.1–8"
# The text
rec:
Her name | *Celeste* is from the Latin *caelestis*, "heavenly." It has no biblical source. She's the only person in the house without scripture in her name, and the one who taught the others theirs.
Lois · 2 Timothy 1.5 | POSSIBLE: Paul names Timothy's grandmother: the "unfeigned faith… which dwelt first in thy grandmother Lois." A grandmother's faith passed down a household. **Where it breaks:** Lois's faith is honored by an apostle in writing. Celeste's is kept off every record on purpose.
Naomi · Ruth 1–4 | POSSIBLE: A woman who's buried what she loved and sends the younger one where the future is. **Where it breaks:** Naomi sends Ruth to a man. Celeste sends Eden toward one and arms her against him in the same sentence (TL).
Rizpah · 2 Samuel 21 | POSSIBLE: She guards the bodies of her sons on a rock from harvest until rain, until the king buries them properly. Celeste has buried three people the old way (DOM). **Where it breaks:** Rizpah's vigil is public and shames a king. Celeste's is arranged so no king ever hears of it.
tab:
| She'd quote | Never read to her in the Temple |
| "Ye are the salt of the earth" (Matt 5.13), at a Salt Ceremony | Isaiah 10.1–2, woe to those who decree unrighteous decrees "that widows may be their prey" |
| "How shall we sing the LORD's song in a strange land?" (Ps 137.4) | Luke 18.1–8, the widow who wears down a judge who "feared not God, neither regarded man" |
| "Pray to thy Father which is in secret" (Matt 6.6) | _ |
box: Volume One
Ruth and Naomi, {v1:ruth} and 109. The unnamed, and two widows, 388. The women who ran things, with the widows of Acts 6, 395. Deuteronomy 6, the household is the school, 334. Lived religion and the Salt Ceremony, 498. Faith that isn't performance, 503. Living memory, 467.
/end

@page rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · CELESTE · BODY AND FEAR" m="TKC: DR3 · TL · DOM"
# Body and fear
Sixty-nine years of holding still in rooms that were assessing her. The manuscript already knows what that looks like.
rec:
What the record shows | A cane she didn't need last summer. This year she says people move faster when you carry one, and both reasons are true (DR3). Back straight in the widows' row, gloved hands over the handle. Three days of not speaking after the reclassification cleared (TL).
What politeness costs | Bell takes her hand and she looks at their joined hands before she answers. The cane shifts against the floor when he says Eden's name (DR3). INTERPRETATION: Her body objects about half a second before her manners arrive, and only Eden can read it.
The heat | Aurem summer "sits on your back from May through October and expects you to carry it" (DOM). She's carried sixty-nine of them.
The three burials | She's buried three people the old way and intends to be buried that way herself (DOM).
What she's afraid is true | INFERENCE: Teaching a girl to keep part of herself unregistered might only be a slower loss. Miriam had the salt water and left anyway. If that's true, she's sent Eden to the Crownward with a hymn instead of armor.
prompt: What her body does that she has no idea Eden can read | fill
prompt: The night she was most afraid, and what she did instead of saying so | fill

