@page rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · CELESTE · VOICE" m="TKC: DR3 · TL"
# Her voice
Hers is the most distinct voice in the manuscript: short, dry, flat, and almost never the answer to the question asked. Lines already on the page are printed. Keep the rhythm when you add to them.
rec:
On the page | "You need to stop bending that card. You distracting me." Without turning her head (DR3).
To a minister | "Morning." To a man of thirty-four years who's just said what a joy it is to see her (DR3).
To the House | "Well. The House calls whom it calls." (DR3)
Teasing | "You like seeing how close you can get." "To what?" "My cane." (DR3)
The receipt | "You paid ten dollars for the right to be judged by people who already decided what you are." (DR3)
Grading | "Passing ain't the same as placing." (DR3)
The last night | "Remember what I told you about the real name of this city." (TL)
small: INFERENCE: Four words where six would do. She answers the second question, not the first, says the hard sentence flat and stops, and never raises her voice in a building the Empire owns.
prompt: Lines only she would say. Write ten, keep three. | fill
prompt: Words she won't use, and why | 3

@page rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · CELESTE · CONTRADICTION" m="TKC: DR3 · TL · DOM"
# The contradiction
Your files contain hers twice, and the cost is on record both times.
rec:
What she believes and breaks | INFERENCE: You don't hand the Empire anything it hasn't already taken. She told Eden exactly what the ten dollars bought, and by midnight she'd cleared a shelf for the study materials (DR3). Knowing better, she helped.
What it cost, the first time | She raised Miriam to refuse everything, and Miriam accepted a Binding at twenty (TL). The Salt Ceremony didn't save her (DOM).
What it may cost now | She's helped the second one out the door toward the same institution in a better dress. Whatever comes after the Trials, she signed for it too.
prompt: Why she helped anyway, in her words and not yours | fill
prompt: The scene where the contradiction lands on her, and the chapter | 4

@essay rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · CELESTE · THE MACHINERY AROUND HER"
# The machinery around her
drop: Five systems press on a sixty-nine-year-old woman with no legal standing, and none of them asks her permission. Read this page, then turn it while it's still loud.
The authority line comes first. Every form that matters asks for an adult male. She wrote "deceased" and it came back circled (DR3). Her authority is real inside the house and gone at the counter, and the Empire will keep sending her paper that says so.
Solomon's scores come next. A common boy whose scores come back exceptional gets a visit from a Crown education officer within three months, and refusing the offer routes him to the worst labor for life (MEN). Two informal notes are already filed, and she's coaching him to show less (MEN). Leah sits under the Commonwife timeline, nearly two years Bound with no pregnancy and a Vowed officer making comments (MEN). Celeste can feed her and can't protect her.
The Temple she attends anyway knows her best and priced her most exactly. Thirty-four years of pie and coats bought her a seat in the widows' row and no sponsorship (DR3). And Aurem itself, the Dominion with the highest Commonwife rate, made marriage look like the highest honor available on purpose (DOM). INTERPRETATION: Staying Unbound in Aurem has never been a private choice. She's been making a public statement for fifty years.
side:
chips: DR3, MEN, DOM
k: Five pressures
stext: The authority line, Solomon's scores, Leah's timeline, the Temple and Aurem.
k: What isn't on the list
stext: Miriam, and every one of the five runs around the hole she left.
marg
see: Vol. I, the forms, 490. Men and labor, 492.
/end

@page rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · CELESTE · EXCAVATION · THE ONE GOOD BLOUSE" m="TKC: TL; WRITING"
# The one good blouse
Anywhere else it would have been called an adoption. Here it took six years, because approval meant proving on paper that her daughter wasn't coming back. She put on her one good blouse, went downtown, said what she needed to say, and didn't speak for three days after it was approved. Eden was in the room when she got home. Celeste held her face in both hands and said nothing (TL).
prompt: 1 · What she said at the counter. Write the sentence that ends a daughter on paper, and what the clerk did with his pen. | fill
prompt: 2 · The three days. The house keeps running. Who cooks, who reads her silence, and what a thirteen-year-old Eden decides it means. | fill
prompt: 3 · Where this shows up later, and what she does differently because of it | 3

@page rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · CELESTE · ONE ORDINARY HOUR" m="WRITING; FREEWRITE"
# Write her alone
Ten minutes of Celeste in that house with nobody in it. No grandchildren to be steady for, no minister to be polite to, no forms. She's performed composure for fifty years, and there's a version of her only an empty kitchen has met.
small: If you stall, reach for one: what she does with the cane when nobody can see · the chair she never sits in · what she cooks for herself and whether she eats it · what she says out loud to God when it's only God · the drawer nothing's come out of in fourteen years · what she does with Miriam's name · the sound of the house at nine at night · what she's still angry about from 2044.
prompt: Write | fill
small: Anything here that surprised you is canon unless you cross it out. Date it.

@page rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · CELESTE AND MIRIAM · BOTH SIDES" m="TKC: TL · FR 3 · FR 6–9 · FR 15–18"
# Celeste and Miriam
note: A daughter's disappearance and a mother's grief.
cols:
k: What Celeste has
Miriam took the first Binding offered and was unrecognizable within four years (TL). Her registration was deactivated in 2090 with no forwarding Dominion (TL). One promise to come back, then nothing, and no letter ever reached the house (FR 12, 16).
prompt: What Celeste thinks happened to her daughter | 7
||
k: What happened
After Thomas left, Miriam was reassigned to another man's household (FR 0). She left her children with Celeste to protect them from that household and from the search (FR 6, 9). She went looking for her father, who was close to the Vanes, and being unreachable wasn't something she chose (FR 15, 17).
prompt: What Miriam would say about her mother's teaching, given the chance | 7
/end
prompt: If Miriam comes back, who's in the room, and what's cooking | fill

